---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="Website privacy notice"
  description="What this website collects: contact form messages and your theme choice. Step runs locally and stores no conversation. No analytics, advertising or tracking cookies."
  path="/website-privacy"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-head-stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Website privacy notice</p>
        <h1 class="display display--page">What this site collects, exactly.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A short notice about this site specifically, separate from the Discord bot's Privacy
          Policy. It is written to describe what actually happens, including the one form.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="notice notice--neutral" role="note" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <span class="status status--planned">Scope</span>
        <p class="notice-body">
          This notice covers <strong>nextstepstudios website</strong> pages only. The
          <a class="text-link" href="/policies/bot/privacy">Discord Bot Privacy Policy</a>
          covers the bot, and is a separate document.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-layout" style="grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);">
        <article class="prose">
          <h2>The short version</h2>
          <p>
            There are no accounts and no analytics. The only information the site collects is
            what you type into the contact form, plus the theme you choose to look at it in.
            Step, the assistant, processes your questions in the browser and does not collect
            them. Nothing is sold, shared for advertising, or used to build a profile.
          </p>

          <h2>The contact form</h2>
          <p>When you send a message, the form records:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>the name you enter;</li>
            <li>the email address you enter, used to reply;</li>
            <li>the subject you choose and the message you write;</li>
            <li>the time the message arrived;</li>
            <li>a one-way hash of your network address, used only to rate-limit repeated submissions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The raw network address is not stored with the message. The hash cannot be turned
            back into an address by the studio.
          </p>
          <p>
            Messages are stored by a small contact service run for the studio, not by an
            advertising or analytics provider. They are kept while the matter they concern is
            open and deleted from the inbox once it is resolved, or sooner if you ask. A message
            can be deleted individually, permanently, from the inbox. There is no automated
            expiry yet, and this notice will say so when there is one.
          </p>
          <p>
            To have a message deleted, send a new message or email
            <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> with enough detail
            to identify it. See <a href="/contact#privacy-requests">privacy requests</a>.
          </p>

          <h2>Your theme choice</h2>
          <p>
            The light/dark toggle stores one value in your browser's local storage under the
            name <span class="mono">nss-theme</span>. It records only “light” or “dark”. It is
            never sent to the server, and clearing site data removes it.
          </p>

          <h2>Step, the local assistant</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Step</strong> is the assistant in the bottom-right corner. When it opens,
            it loads two static files from this site: a curated fact file and the same
            full-text index used by the Search page. Your question is matched against that
            content in your browser.
          </p>
          <p>
            The question is not sent to an AI service, a model or an API, and it is not put in
            a URL. The conversation is held in memory only: it is not written to local
            storage or a cookie, and it is discarded when you reload or close the page. The
            static-file requests are ordinary page requests and do not contain your question.
          </p>
          <p>
            Step can offer links to public pages and to the contact form. If it cannot find
            an answer in the public pages, it says so rather than inventing one.
          </p>

          <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
          <p>Specifically, the website does not use:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>analytics, statistics or audience-measurement services;</li>
            <li>advertising, advertising pixels or affiliate tracking;</li>
            <li>session recording, heatmaps or fingerprinting;</li>
            <li>third-party embeds, widgets or social plugins;</li>
            <li>third-party fonts, scripts or content delivery networks;</li>
            <li>tracking cookies. Visitors receive no cookies from this site at all.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The fonts are served from the site itself, and every page is a pre-built file. The
            private contact inbox is different: it sets one session cookie, and only after the
            operator signs in. Visitors never receive it.
          </p>

          <h2>The status feed</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/status">status page</a> asks GitHub for a small JSON file that records
            the bot's recent checks. That request goes to <span class="mono">raw.githubusercontent.com</span>,
            not to this site, and it is the only request a page makes to another host on its own.
            The contact form talks to the studio's contact service only when you submit it. GitHub
            necessarily receives your network address, browser user agent and the time of the
            request to serve it. The page sends no cookie and no identifier of its own. If the
            request fails, the page falls back to the copy of the file built with the site.
          </p>

          <h2>Hosting and technical logs</h2>
          <p>
            The site is served by a hosting provider, which necessarily processes network
            requests to deliver pages. Like most hosting, this may involve standard request
            logs — such as the requesting IP address, the browser's user agent, the requested
            page, and a timestamp — kept by the provider for delivery, security and
            troubleshooting.
          </p>
          <p>
            These logs are not used by the studio for profiling, advertising or tracking, and
            this site does not combine them with any other information. The exact logging and
            retention behaviour of the hosting provider is set by that provider rather than by
            this website.
          </p>

          <h2>Email</h2>
          <p>
            If you email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>, the
            studio receives your email address, the contents of your message, and anything you
            choose to attach, and uses them to read and answer your message. Email is handled
            by the third-party email provider that hosts that mailbox, under its own terms.
          </p>

          <h2>What this notice does not claim</h2>
          <p>
            This notice describes what the website actually does. It is not a legal opinion and
            does not claim compliance with any particular law or regulation. If the site later
            adds a feature that collects new information — accounts, analytics, a different
            form — this notice will be rewritten before that feature goes live.
          </p>

          <h2>Changes</h2>
          <p>
            This notice is updated when the website's behaviour changes. It carries no version
            history; it is revised whenever a feature changes what the site collects or sends.
          </p>

          <h2>Contact</h2>
          <p>
            Questions about this notice, or about the website, go to
            <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. Requests
            concerning information held by the Discord bot are handled through the same
            address — see <a href="/contact#privacy-requests">privacy requests</a>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
